title: Realistic Energy
message: If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from this file.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Jonathan
    family-names: Davidson
    affiliation: Vrije Universiteit
repository-code: "https://github.com/J-Davidson01/realistic-energy"
url: "https://github.com/J-Davidson01/realistic-energy"
abstract: >-
  The increasing reliance on software-driven systems contributes to rising energy
  demand, placing pressure on energy grids and computing infrastructure. In
  large-scale systems, even small improvements in software energy efficiency can
  lead to meaningful reductions in total energy consumption. Since software
  design and implementation influence how hardware resources are used,
  programming language choice can affect the energy efficiency of applications.

  Existing studies on programming language energy efficiency often rely on small
  synthetic benchmarks or isolated algorithmic tasks. Such benchmarks are useful
  for controlled comparison, but may not fully capture how applications behave
  when CPU-oriented, memory-oriented, and I/O-oriented stages are combined. This
  limits how directly their conclusions can be applied to realistic
  data-processing workloads.

  This thesis addresses this gap by designing and evaluating a reproducible
  benchmark suite for C, Go, Julia, and Python. The benchmark suite is based on
  recurring stages in ETL-inspired data-processing pipelines: JSON parsing,
  byte-buffer processing, sequential file output, and composed pipeline
  execution. The workloads are evaluated in isolation and as composed end-to-end
  pipelines using runtime, CPU package energy, DRAM energy, and peak resident
  set size as metrics.

  The results show that no single language is best for all workloads and
  metrics. C performs strongly in most runtime and CPU package energy
  measurements, while Go generally remains close to C across workloads. Python
  often shows low peak memory usage, but performs poorly when large byte-wise
  loops are executed at interpreter level. Julia shows a larger fixed runtime
  and memory footprint in simple workloads but performs very well when compiled
  byte-buffer loops are optimized. The results also show that library choice and
  implementation details can substantially affect measured behavior, as seen in
  the JSON parser variants and the SHA-256/XOR vectorization behavior. Overall,
  this thesis shows that realistic energy benchmarking should compare
  representative implementations of workload classes rather than treating
  programming languages as absolute rankings.
